  "tour": {
    "title": "When Prayer Won’t Come: Grief, Silence, and the Presence of God",
    "intro": "I’m so sorry about your mother. This tour begins with a person speaking from the rawness of losing someone he loved, then makes room for silence before moving toward honest prayer: Jesus’ grief, the Bible’s language of lament, and the possibility that even wordlessness is heard. It is meant to keep you company, not hurry you toward a tidy answer.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "radically-christian:podcast:a9baf6f-substack-post-151262658",
        "startSec": 568,
        "endSec": 836,
        "why": "Start with company rather than explanation. After describing his wife’s death, the speaker says, “my life had changed forever and it would never be the same again,” that he “miss[es] my wife every single day,” and that there are times when he feels “very alone.” His account stays honest about grief remaining “difficult every single day,” without suggesting that three weeks—or any fixed span—should be enough.",
        "episodeTitle": "Some Things I Wish Everyone Knew About Grief",
        "sourceId": "radically-christian",
        "source": "Radically Christian",
        "audioUrl": "https://substackcdn.com/video_upload/post/151262658/96f24d4c-c85d-4c44-be4e-c5eb9605780e/transcoded.mp3?post_id=151262658&relation=podcast&Expires=1785437767&Key-Pair-Id=APKAIVDA3NPSMPSPESQQ&Signature=dgjzRqPzmoQPsEMSSMmExZhFh7Emn1byXf2jYT6tjJfQFCFY-MJhSeKje9oF9yQR6ly~pvN1A7E12joCs~tO2qo8wwrEp-87JE2tJillnoBH9tG2T~vqqrabTIR6AdgW9O1-HGJVcgv9OVpczgso6y2Ri5ZnF0ART0pnBzK28lyaFeUtw9Buzzt-BikeYnkKonRgv5N718fw0QNC5UqCh9kxvpTBaglQo3U3SDJEOOq41EdD9NctwYz~vIs77WEjNb2udS0EyLq5rnBeverhs6wtqKpOIBXOrYrLbQ8UGz~wbkysHH2ZxU3m9VS3b1MH74yAddxl3ocINNbGt5ua~g__",
        "durationSec": 3241,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:444302a2-d904-43b3-a21f-4c7fb0a30dd7",
        "startSec": 620,
        "endSec": 764,
        "why": "Let this be a short lesson in what love can look like when there are no useful words. Discussing Job’s friends, the hosts call silent companionship “the ministry of presence,” warn against arriving with “prepared words of consolation and comfort,” and say, of one friend’s death, “it wasn’t the words much that mattered, it just was they were there.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Job 2:11-3:26 (Episode 387)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_ep_387.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2435,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:f189e210-8df4-11ee-b97f-09218d3fce01",
        "startSec": 136,
        "endSec": 370,
        "why": "This directly meets the feeling that prayer is impossible or that your grief is too small to bring to God. Responding to a grieving daughter who feels prayer is “silly” and her broken heart “selfish and unimportant,” Wright says, “this is not selfish and it is not unimportant,” and, “God loves your broken heart.” He points her toward Jesus’ grief and even Psalm 88, which he says “leaves you in the dark,” rather than requiring quick resolution.",
        "episodeTitle": "#196 Pain and suffering Part 2 (Classic)",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR3879333769.mp3?updated=1749653312",
        "durationSec": 1455,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:67576c87-242c-42e5-a03d-63663cd5d2d6",
        "startSec": 1399,
        "endSec": 1670,
        "why": "Now hear lament treated as real prayer, not spiritual failure. The speakers say Psalm 22 gives a congregation words “to dignify their suffering,” and that confidence in God’s goodness can exist alongside “doubts and pain, sorrow, and confusion.” They linger over the modest request, “Be not far from me,” and describe God as inviting “a prolonged conversation”—a way of bringing what hurts without disguising it.",
        "episodeTitle": "Psalm 22: Lament Psalms (w/ Cameron Cole)",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG7303573431.mp3?updated=1686154567",
        "durationSec": 2801,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:8f69bf0a-com-59af7369-e75d-395e-85a6-34ac29b8e25e",
        "startSec": 150,
        "endSec": 440,
        "why": "End here with permission not to manufacture words. The commentator says, “Sometimes it’s just like, ‘I don’t even know what to say,’” then explains Paul’s phrase “wordless groanings” and says that, in such a moment, the Spirit “takes what we’re groaning about.” For now, this offers no prayer technique—only the possibility that silence, breath, tears, and groaning are not outside the conversation.",
        "episodeTitle": "Romans 8:26-39",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/8rp82y/romans_826-39blqmq.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1393,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "You do not have to finish this tour with a conclusion or a prayer. If all you can do today is listen, cry, sit in silence, or borrow someone else’s words, let that be enough for today. These voices do not erase your mother or explain the timing of her death; they leave room for protest, tears, absence, and the hope that God may meet you there—not first as an answer, but as presence.",
    "totalSeconds": 1207
